 "notes": "Libretto by Luigi Illica and Giuseppe Giacosa (1896). Words follow the Ricordi vocal score's spelling and punctuation (Niuno, l'april, lascieremo beside lascerem, aspettiam, Chiacchieran, stagion, fior, verno, Chè mi canti? with the print's accent, Fo'all'amor, vò; Rodolfo's \"e cosa da morire\" without the accent of Mimì's \"è\"; the print's runs of dots, e.g. after finita, vita, poeta, inverno, l'april, stende, fior, mattina, mariti, cappello, ornamenti, incivettire). \"Fo'all'amor\" (Musetta, bars 54 and 56) is printed as one run, Fo'all' on one note: it is given as two words, Fo' + all'amor. The first bar (figure (29)) is shared with the previous piece: it holds Mimì's last syllable \"-cor\" of \"senza rancor\", which belongs to \"Donde lieta uscì\" and is left out of this piece's phrases; Rodolfo's line starts there. Musetta's \"Pittore da bottega! Rospo!\" and Marcello's \"Vipera! strega!\" are shouted (gridato), printed without noteheads, and kept as sung words. IPA follows sung Italian conventions: stressed syllable marked, words sung on one note joined with ‿, open e/o as ɛ/ɔ, intervocalic s voiced; io and ch'io on one note are jo, kjo. Translation literal, for meaning.",
